@page "/terms"

<SeoHead Title="Terms of Service — CrossPlatformDev"
         Description="Terms governing website use and project engagements with CrossPlatformDev."
         Path="/terms" />

<div class="page-enter section">
    <div class="container" style="max-width: 820px;">
        <span class="eyebrow">terms</span>
        <h1>Terms of Service</h1>
        <p class="text-muted">Last updated: @LastUpdated</p>

        <div class="legal-body">
            <p>
                These terms cover two things: general use of this website, and the standard terms that apply
                when you engage me for development work. Specific projects are further governed by a signed
                agreement (like a Memorandum of Understanding or contract) between us, which takes precedence
                over the general terms below where they differ.
            </p>

            <h2>1. Website Use</h2>
            <p>
                This site and its content are provided for informational purposes about CrossPlatformDev's
                services. You may browse, read, and contact me freely. Content on this site (text, design,
                code snippets shown as examples) may not be copied or reused for commercial purposes without
                permission.
            </p>

            <h2>2. Project Engagements — General Terms</h2>
            <p>
                When you hire me for a website, mobile app, or business system, the following generally applies
                unless a specific written agreement says otherwise:
            </p>

            <h3>Scope of Work</h3>
            <p>
                Every project starts with an agreed scope — what's being built, the technologies used, and
                what's included. Changes outside that scope may involve additional cost and timeline, discussed
                before any extra work begins.
            </p>

            <h3>Payment</h3>
            <p>
                Projects are typically paid in staged deposits tied to milestones (e.g. 25% at key project
                stages — database/login setup, core interface completion, project completion, and final
                hosting/launch), rather than one lump sum. Exact payment terms are set per project and confirmed
                in writing before work begins. Accepted payment methods include e-transfer and cash.
            </p>

            <h3>Third-Party Costs</h3>
            <p>
                Costs for domains, hosting, and any required third-party APIs are separate from the development
                fee and are the client's responsibility, paid either directly to the provider or reimbursed to
                me if I set them up on your behalf.
            </p>

            <h3>Client Responsibilities</h3>
            <p>
                Clients are expected to provide necessary materials on time — content, product data, images,
                business information, and access to accounts needed to complete the project. Delays in
                providing these may extend the project timeline accordingly.
            </p>

            <h3>Ownership</h3>
            <p>
                Upon full and final payment, ownership of the custom code developed for your project transfers
                to you. Reusable components, frameworks, or general-purpose tools I've built independently
                remain mine and may be reused in future projects.
            </p>

            <h3>Revisions & Support</h3>
            <p>
                Reasonable revisions during development are included as part of the agreed scope. Ongoing
                support, feature additions, or maintenance after launch are available separately and discussed
                per project.
            </p>

            <h3>Project Timelines</h3>
            <p>
                Timelines are estimates based on the agreed scope and assume timely client feedback and
                materials. Delays caused by the client (late content, slow feedback, scope changes) may extend
                the delivery date.
            </p>

            <h3>Cancellation</h3>
            <p>
                If a client becomes unresponsive for an extended period (generally two weeks or more) without
                communication, the project may be considered paused or ended without refund of deposits already
                paid for completed work.
            </p>

            <h2>3. Confidentiality</h2>
            <p>
                Any confidential business information shared during a project — trade secrets, business plans,
                financial details, or client data — is kept confidential during and after our working
                relationship, and is not disclosed to third parties without consent.
            </p>

            <h2>4. Limitation of Liability</h2>
            <p>
                I build software carefully and test before delivery, but I'm not liable for indirect losses,
                lost profits, or damages arising from use of the delivered software beyond the fees paid for
                the project. Clients are responsible for maintaining their own backups and reviewing delivered
                work before relying on it in production.
            </p>

            <h2>5. Dispute Resolution</h2>
            <p>
                If a disagreement arises during a project, both parties agree to first attempt to resolve it
                through direct discussion and mutual agreement before pursuing other remedies.
            </p>

            <h2>6. Governing Law</h2>
            <p>These terms are governed by the laws of Ontario, Canada.</p>

            <h2>7. Contact</h2>
            <p>
                Questions about these terms? Reach out at
                <a href="mailto:aliabdulsameea69@gmail.com">aliabdulsameea69@gmail.com</a> or
                <a href="tel:+12893391367">+1 289 339 1367</a>.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<style>
    .legal-body {
        margin-top: var(--space-6);
        color: var(--text-secondary);
        line-height: 1.8;
    }

        .legal-body h2 {
            font-size: 1.4rem;
            margin-top: var(--space-7);
            margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
        }

        .legal-body h3 {
            font-size: 1.05rem;
            margin-top: var(--space-5);
            margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
            color: var(--text-primary);
        }

        .legal-body p {
            margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
        }

        .legal-body strong {
            color: var(--text-primary);
        }
</style>

@code {
    private string LastUpdated => DateTime.Now.ToString("MMMM d, yyyy");
}